local patches (Sources/CLibArchive/patches, applied in the vendored tree; reverse with patch -p1 -R)
  0001-write-disk-no-appledouble-fixup.patch: write_disk extracts "._" AppleDouble files raw
  0002-dos-time-memo.patch: __archive_dos_to_unix memoizes the last conversion per thread
  0003-utf8-names-stay-nfc-on-apple.patch: setup_converter keeps UTF-8 -> UTF-8 names NFC on macOS (upstream converts to NFD)
  0004-7zip-aes-read.patch: the 7zip reader decrypts 7zAES folders and encrypted headers (per-packed-stream AES-256-CBC via kernir_7zaes)
  0005-7zip-aes-write.patch: the 7zip writer encrypts every folder and the header with 7zAES (option encryption=aes256; random IV per folder via kernir_7zaes)
  0006-gzip-trailer-check-tar-reads-to-end.patch: the gzip filter checks each member's trailer CRC32 and ISIZE (ARCHIVE_FATAL on mismatch); tar reads past its end-of-archive blocks to the end of the stream so a filter's trailer check runs
  0007-7zip-lha-data-crc-fails.patch: a data CRC mismatch is ARCHIVE_FAILED in the 7zip reader (plain folders too) and the LHA reader, not ARCHIVE_WARN
  0009-zip-name-charset-hooks.patch: kernir_read_zip_entry_name_declared (bit 11 or a used 0x7075 field) and kernir_write_zip_next_name_raw (next archive_write_header, consumed at its start in archive_write.c, stores MBS name bytes verbatim, bit 11 as given); adds include/kernir_names.h
  0010-rar4-name-unicode-accessor.patch: kernir_read_rar_entry_name_declared (1 when the RAR4 reader took the name as Unicode: FHD_UNICODE with decoded UTF-16, or no NUL so UTF-8; 0 for OEM names and when not reading RAR4; reset at the start of every header read and after a failing file header); declared in include/kernir_names.h (applies on top of 0009)
  0011-zip64-locator-zero-disks.patch: read_zip64_eocd accepts a zip64 locator with total disks 0 (OneDrive/SharePoint downloads over 4 GB) like 1; more than 1 is still refused
  0012-zip-comments.patch: zip comments as raw bytes: kernir_read_zip_entry_comment (the seekable reader keeps each central-directory comment; 0 for the streaming reader), kernir_write_zip_next_comment (next archive_write_header, taken at its start in archive_write.c like 0009, appended to the central-directory record after the extra fields) and kernir_write_zip_archive_comment (written after the end record at close); the seekable bidder searches the last 20 + 22 + 65535 bytes for the end record (upstream's rule in the last 16 KiB; further back only a record whose comment reaches EOF), so archive comments over 16 KiB keep the seekable reader; declared in include/kernir_names.h (applies on top of 0009)
  0013-write-free-ends-compressors.patch: freeing a write handle without close (state FATAL, after archive_write_fail) ends the gzip and xz filters' streams (free_data calls deflateEnd / lzma_end, no-ops on an ended or never-started stream) and the zip writer's live per-entry compressor (kernir_stream_live: set when write_header starts it, cleared when finish_entry ends it; ended in free and before the next header); upstream ended them only in close, so every cancelled or failed tar.gz, tar.xz or zip-deflate save kept the codec state (applies on top of 0012)
  0014-7zip-name-limits.patch: the 7zip reader's read_Header refuses an entry name over 65,536 UTF-16 units while it copies the kName block, and a kName block over 512 MiB plus 2 bytes per entry before it allocates it (ARCHIVE_FATAL, ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT, own messages that slurp_central_directory keeps: KERNIR_7Z_REFUSED); both limits sit above what the caller lists (64 KiB of UTF-8 per name, 256 MiB of UTF-8 names and link targets per archive); upstream copied the whole block before the first header, so a 57 KB 7z cost 597 MB (applies on top of 0007)
  0015-xar-toc-limits.patch: the xar reader refuses, while read_toc parses the TOC, an entry nested more than 200 levels deep (bare folders too; in file_new, before it is allocated), a pathname (parent prefix included) or symlink target over 65,536 bytes, and stored pathnames plus symlink targets plus the hardlink target copies of the connect loop (charged before they are made) over 256 MiB (ARCHIVE_FATAL, ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT, own messages; sets the xar format so the caller sees a damaged xar; with expat a callback error is kept when XML_Parse fails later in the same chunk); all limits sit above what the caller lists (200 components, 16 KiB per path, 256 MiB of names and link targets per archive); upstream built every full pathname before the first header, so a 14 KB xar of 4,000 nested folders cost 2.6 GB
  0016-entry-count-limits.patch: kernir_read_set_name_limits / kernir_read_get_name_limits (struct archive_read; defaults 4,000,000 entries, 256 MiB, 512 bytes per entry = NestingLimits.standard; declared in include/kernir_names.h); the 7zip reader refuses numFiles, folders and unpack streams over the entry limit and pack streams over 4 times it (saturated), before allocating them, and keeps 0014's names block limit fixed at 2 × the names limit + 2 × numFiles (512 MiB + 2 B per entry: a claimed numFiles must not grow it); the xar reader refuses <file> and <ea> elements over the entry limit and more than 1,024 <ea> on one file (xattr_new's sorted insert walks the list: quadratic), and grows 0015's names total to max(256 MiB, 512 × the <file> elements so far); refusals keep their own messages (applies on top of 0015)
  0017-xar-name-chardata.patch: the xar reader joins the pieces expat hands over for one <name> or <link> text (split at entity and character references such as &amp;, at newlines and at each 64 KiB chunk of inflated TOC; upstream kept the first piece of a name and the last of a link target, so "Tom &amp; Jerry.txt" read as "Tom "; libarchive#3849): only an entry's first <name> counts, the last <link> counts whole, the parent prefix is added once, and 0015's per-name and per-link limit and 0016's names total are charged on the joined text (applies on top of 0016)
  0018-xar-name-decode-once.patch: the xar reader collects a <name enctype="base64"> raw (whitespace left out, refused over 87,384 characters: 65,536 bytes' worth) and decodes it once at </name>, where 0015's per-name limit and 0016's names total are charged on the decoded name with the parent prefix, so a name whose pieces end inside a 4-character group (a 64 KiB TOC chunk boundary, a line break, a character reference) decodes whole (0017 decoded each piece: "QUJ" + "D" read "AB"); an <ea>'s <name> joins its pieces (upstream kept the last one, so x&amp;y read "y"; the last <name> counts) within the 65,536-byte per-name limit; xml_end returns an int so the XML callbacks pass a refusal on; base64 decoding stops at the first "=" (strappend_base64), so a name padded per line ("QQ==" newline "Qg==") reads "A" where 0017 read "AB" (libarchive and xar pad only at the end); libarchive#3849 (applies on top of 0017)
  0019-xar-bare-folders-entry-cap.patch: the xar reader counts against the entry limit only the <file> elements xar_read_header returns (counted at </file>; a bare folder, a directory with only a name and a type, is never returned and no longer counts, so it matches the headers Kernir counts), holds every <file>, bare folders included, to 2 × the entry limit (saturated) before allocating it, and grows 0016's names total with at most the entry limit's worth of <file> elements (__archive_read_kernir_names_allowance caps its count, as Kernir's allowances do; the names total still charges bare folders, so it can refuse slightly before Swift) (applies on top of 0018)
  0020-xar-ea-names-total.patch: the xar reader charges an <ea>'s <name> to 0016's names total by its joined length, as pathnames and link targets are (refused past it with 0016's message; a replaced <name>, the last one counting, is no longer charged), so many or long <ea> names no longer escape the names limit; one over 65,536 bytes is refused with its own message, "xar extended attribute name is longer than 65536 bytes" (0018 reused the entry-name one), which Kernir maps to the same path-length limit (applies on top of 0019)
  0021-iso9660-directory-limits.patch: the iso9660 reader reads a directory's extent one logical block at a time (upstream read ahead the whole extent, whose size the image claims: a 296 KiB image claiming a 4 GiB directory had libarchive malloc 4.3 GB before EOF; choose_volume did the same with the root's size and now reads only the root's record, at most 255 bytes), counts every directory record (the root included; multi-extent parts, CL placeholders and a hidden rr_moved too, so such an image can be refused slightly before Swift) against 0016's entry limit before allocating it, and charges each record's Rockridge name and link target after the record and after each continuation area (several records' CE entries can share one CE chain, which grew every one of their names: an 848 KB image built 583 MB of names before the first header), refusing one over 65,536 bytes and all of them over 0016's names total, and charges each record's pass over each continuation area by the area's size to a total of its own held to the same allowance plus the bytes read so far (a valid image's distinct areas, such as AAIP ACL and xattr entries, fit in those; a chain growing no name still cost N x k parses, CPU quadratic in the image's size) (ARCHIVE_FATAL, ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT, own messages: "ISO9660 image has more entries than the entry limit", "ISO9660 entry name is longer than 65536 bytes", "ISO9660 link target is longer than 65536 bytes", "ISO9660 entry names and link targets take more than the names limit"); include/kernir_names.h notes it (applies on top of 0020)
  0022-zip-central-directory-limits.patch: the seekable zip reader holds its central directory to 0016's limits while slurp_central_directory reads it, before the first header: records with a new local header offset (the headers it returns) to the entry limit, every record (repeated offsets included, which are never returned) to 2 × it (saturated) before it is allocated, and the record names to max(256 MiB, 512 × the listed records so far) (ARCHIVE_FATAL, ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT, own messages, mapped to Kernir's entry and names limits); it no longer parses the records' Info-ZIP Unicode Path fields (0x7075) against the entry being returned (unused there: the local header sets the name), whose stale pathname each field re-hashed; the names are charged in central directory order against the listed records so far, without link targets (Kernir charges the headers in local header offset order, link targets included), so a crafted central directory whose long names come first can be refused though Kernir would take its headers; and read_zip64_eocd bids only when the zip64 central directory's offset is at or before the zip64 end record (its size, which nothing reads, is not checked); upstream allocated every record whatever the limit, so a 23 MB zip of 500,000 records held 120 MB, and an 11 MB zip of 0x7075 fields cost 5.3 s of CPU, before the first header (applies on top of 0020)
  0023-apple-current-charset-utf8.patch: on __APPLE__ default_iconv_charset returns "UTF-8" when no charset is cached, so every handle and every entry (archive_entry_clear unbinds header entries from their archive) converts names with UTF-8 as the current-locale charset whatever the thread's LC_CTYPE; iOS 17 has no UTF-8 locale (newlocale fails, "C" is US-ASCII) and non-ASCII names failed to convert; the multibyte <-> wide conversions behind archive_entry_*_w and invalid_mbs use libarchive's own UTF-8 decoder and encoder instead of mbrtowc/wcrtomb (in "C" each byte was one wide character, so the zip and cab readers split CP932 names at a 0x5C trail byte)
  0024-iso9660-ce-high-water.patch: the iso9660 reader holds 0021's continuation-area total to the names allowance plus the furthest position it has reached (kernir_high_water, raised before read_header's seek back to a file's body, the only place current_position goes back), not current_position: after such a seek (a directory listed after the body it points at, seekable sources only) a directory read below the furthest point had its areas held to the allowance plus its own end, so a valid image could be refused; without a seek back nothing changes, and the bound stays the allowance plus bytes of the image passed (applies on top of 0023)
  0025-zip-comments-names-total.patch: the seekable zip reader charges each central-directory comment it keeps (patch 0012: up to 65,535 bytes per record, held until the reader is freed, so about the central directory's size in all) to 0022's names counter after the record's name, before copying it, against the same allowance (max(names_bytes, name_bytes_per_entry × the listed records so far)); names plus comments over it are refused (ARCHIVE_FATAL, ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT, "ZIP entry comments take the entry names past the names limit", mapped to Kernir's names limit); upstream plus 0012 held a crafted zip's comments, about its own size, whatever the names total (Quick Look: 64 MiB); refused, not dropped, since a zip -> zip save copies them (applies on top of 0024)
